Streamlit-based IT support ticket dashboard for tracking, filtering and creating incidents, with CI through Azure Pipelines and deployment to Azure Web App.

Suggested topics: python streamlit pandas azure-pipelines azure-web-app ci-cd dashboard it-support ticketing-system

README description
IT Support Ticket Dashboard

A web dashboard built with Python, Streamlit and pandas for monitoring and managing IT support incidents. It is deployed on Azure App Service (Web App), and an Azure Pipelines CI pipeline builds and tests every push.

Features
Ticket summary: metric cards show total tickets and counts for Open, In Progress, Resolved and Closed.
Filtering and search: filter by status and priority, or search by ticket ID or title.
Incident details: select a ticket to see its title, priority, status, assignee and description.
Ticket creation: a form takes a title, description, priority, initial status and assignee. It validates the title and auto-generates the next ticket ID.
Session state: tickets are held in st.session_state, seeded with sample incidents such as Wi-Fi issues, Outlook problems, password resets and shared drive access.
Tech stack
Area	Tools
Frontend / app	Streamlit
Data handling	pandas
Testing	test_app.py (automated tests)
CI	Azure Pipelines (azure-pipelines.yml)
Hosting	Azure Web App
CI/CD

The Azure Pipelines run is triggered on each push to main and runs on an ubuntu-latest Microsoft-hosted agent. It:

Checks out the repository
Sets up Python
Installs dependencies from requirements.txt
Runs the automated tests

The web app was deployed to Azure through a Web App deployment in its own resource group.

Project structure
├── app.py                 # Streamlit dashboard
├── test_app.py            # Automated tests
├── requirements.txt       # Python dependencies
└── azure-pipelines.yml    # CI pipeline definition
Run locally
bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
streamlit run app.py
Notes
Ticket data is in-memory sample data, so new tickets reset when the session restarts. A database such as SQLite, PostgreSQL or Azure SQL would be a natural next step.

